{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1248223-2-5e-artifact-c-c-the-quick-and-the-dead","id":1248223,"title":"[2.5e] Artifact C&C: The Quick and The Dead","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-08-20T15:04:33-07:00","id":1248223,"title":"[2.5e] Artifact C&C: The Quick and The Dead","content":"A pair of artifact prayer-piece style pistols designed for an Abyssal exalt. Apologies for any typos, or repetition in the mechanics; I only just finished the write-up!<br /> <br /> I'd appreciate any commentary people have; it's currently a 4-dot artifact, but I'm not sure whether people think that rating is right? I'm slightly tempted to add in a 10m and/or 1wp cost power which causes a shot to inflict aggravated damage to the relevant target type, but I don't know if that would be too much for a 4-dot? Would it push it up to 5-dots?<br /> <br /> <b><u>The Quick and the Dead</u></b><br /> Artifact: oooo<br /> <br /> This paired set of artifacts appears to be two exquisitely crafted soulsteel prayer pieces, ornately decorated and polished to a mirror sheen, appearing almost silver in the dim sunlight of the underworld, or the light of Creation's moon. However, a knowledgeable individual who closely examines the weapons will reveal a number of telling differences between them and the standard piece, if indeed such relics of the first age can be considered standard.<br /> <br /> Firstly, their soulsteel construction is incredibly unusual; while some Solars worked this unwholesome material in the high first age, there are no known records of items so classically regarded as being dedicated to Sol Invictus crafted out of so dark a substance, which a difficulty 5 Lore + Intelligence check will reveal. Perhaps they were crafted by a Solar in the first age and concealed out of shame or fear of reprisal? Or even more worryingly, perhaps they were crafted at some later point by a being more resonant with this magical material, but who, since the halcyon days of the past, has the skill and understanding to forge items of such power? Doubtless the answers, one way or the other, lie in the depths of the labyrinth.<br /> <br /> To notice a subtler difference requires examination of the devices' inner workings. Whereas a prayer piece uses the faith and devotion of its least gods to launch its slugs, the necromantically agitating glyphs carved into the barrels of The Quick and The Dead and their ammunition rouse and awaken the spirits bound within its soulsteel construction, driving them into a frenzy of hunger or rage, respectively. This storm of emotions and will becomes bottled behind the round, explosively launching the slug in a burst of vivid pyre-flame, graveyard fog, and the howls of the angry dead. A sufficiently detailed inspection and a Occult + Intelligence check at difficulty 5 (difficulty 3 for those knowledgeable about prayer pieces) reveals the secrets of the weapons' inner workings.<br /> <br /> Much like prayer pieces, The Quick and The Dead can be loaded with ammunition crafted from any soft metal, but actually function best when loaded with base lead, preferably harvested from grave goods or long lost and forgotten sources, such as sunken ships or statues buried in earthquakes. Alternately, any lead harvested from sources in the underworld is also regarded as especially suitable.<br /> <br /> Both The Quick and The Dead have the same stat line, as follows (which includes the magical material bonuses for soulsteel), although they each exhibit different abilities as detailed below. Firing either The Quick or The Dead costs 1 mote per shot, to rouse the bound spirits of the dead and damned into life, apart from in circumstances described below.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Speed</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Acc</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Dmg</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Rate</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Range</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Ammo</b></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><b>Attune</b></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">+5</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">10L**</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">2</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">50</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">6</td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\">8*</td> </tr> </table></div> * The attunement cost is for both pistols, which cannot be attuned separately.<br /> ** Both The Quick and The Dead cause Aggravated damage to raksha and other creatures of the Wyld.<br /> <br /> <u>The Quick</u><br /> The Quick is decorated quite unusually for an artifact of the underworld, its sides and barrel being covered in ornately wrought roses and thorns, and the butt of its grip ending in a stunningly lifelike carving of a rose viewed straight-on, if roses were grown from haunting nightmare steel. However, any who stare too long at the weapon cannot help but see its true nature as a killing tool; the petals on the carved roses seem to constantly fall away to reveal ever more thorns, and the barbs themselves clearly drip with the blood of past and future victims.<br /> <br /> The soulsteel from which The Quick was forged is composed of a horde of hungry ghosts, kept ravenous and unfed and smelted into the material for the weapon at the peak of their hunger. As such, the weapon itself lusts for the essence of life, its ghosts more than happy to spit their fury and hunger at living targets. When fired at such targets, or any target mainly composed of the essence of Creation such as gods or elementals, The Quick does not require the 1 mote surcharge to be fired and returns any motes drained by its soulsteel nature to its wielder, as if drained by the charm Ravening Mouth of (Ability).<br /> <br /> Furthermore, if the wielder chooses to spend 5 motes before firing a round, the shot becomes charged with the desperate hunger of the ghosts bound to the weapon. A shot so-empowered continues to hunt its initial target if the initial attack fails to hit, including if the attack is dodged or parried, attacking on every one of the wielder's actions using the same pool and effects as the original attack. This continues until either the attack hits, the attack roll botches, or the slug itself is attacked, which requires a successful attack roll with an external penalty of Archery + Essence, which must inflict at least 3 lethal damage. This is not limited by range, and the target will continue to be hunted by the attack even if he leaves combat completely and moves outside of the weapon's range.<br /> <br /> <u>The Dead</u><br /> In contrast to its unusually adorned sibling, The Dead appears closer to the normal expectations for a weapon forged from soulsteel, its barrel draped in skeletal fingers, its revolving chambers enclosed in a silver ribcage, and the butt of its grip topped by a grinning skull. While the living find the weapon suitably disquieting, the dead are drawn to its mirror sheen, enraptured half in horror and half in awe. Only when the weapon explodes into a frenzy of violence is their reverie broken.<br /> <br /> The souls bound to the frame of The Dead are those of true ghosts, the higher souls of mortals who had lived long and storied lives in the Underworld. As part of the forging of this dire weapon, each was betrayed by those closest to them and sold into sacrifice at the soulsteel forges, their souls still howling in anguish at their betrayers. The result is a weapon which is all too pleased to direct its deadly fury at its fellow deceased.<br /> <br /> When fired at dead targets, such as ghosts, the walking dead, and stranger beings such as hekatonhires and even the deathlords themselves, The Dead does not demand the 1 mote surcharge to be fired and returns any motes drained by its soulsteel nature to its wielder, as if drained by the charm Ravening Mouth of (Ability).<br /> <br /> Furthermore, if the wielder chooses to spend 5 motes before firing a round, the shot becomes charged with the bitter hatred of the ghosts bound to the weapon. A shot so-empowered continues to hunt its initial target if the initial attack fails to hit, including if the attack is dodged or parried, attacking on every one of the wielder's actions using the same pool and effects as the original attack. This continues until either the attack hits, the attack roll botches, or the slug itself is attacked, which requires a successful attack roll with an external penalty of Archery + Essence, which must inflict at least 3 lethal damage. This is not limited by range, and the target will continue to be hunted by the attack even if he leaves combat completely and moves outside of the weapon's range.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11800-kajata","name":"Kajata","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11800_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}